.SH DESCRIPTION
A graphics driver is a user-space file server on the hardware
that
.IR gpu (3)
hands out.
It posts
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BR gpu.0 )
and mounts itself after
.BR /dev ,
so its files appear as
.BR /dev/gpu/0 .
Control is text; data moves through shared segments, never through
the file server.
Every driver serves the same tree; what a family does not have is
missing.
.PP
.I Virtiogpu
drives virtio-gpu (QEMU's
.BR virtio-gpu-pci ,
.BR virtio-gpu-gl-pci ).
It finds the device's registers in the configuration space, keeps
its virtqueue in a fixed
.IR segment (3),
makes the screen the framebuffer of
.IR gpu (3)
and copies what changes to the host.
.B -p
polls instead of using the interrupt,
.B -m
sets the first mode (default: what the host asks for),
.B -d
writes debugging output to standard error (not to the console:
that draws on the screen, which makes more output).
.SS Display
.TF outputs/conn/modes
.TP
.B ctl
reads the current mode;
.BI "mode " w x h
sets it.
.TP
.B status
the device, its features, the queue, the mode, the framebuffer,
the host visible memory, a context's resource on the display
.RB ( scanout
below) and every context with its fences.
.TP
.BI outputs/ conn /modes
the modes, one
.IB w x h
per line, the preferred first.
.TP
.BI outputs/ conn /mode
reads or sets the mode of the output; the screen
.RI ( vga (3)
type
.BR gpu )
follows it.
.TP
.BI outputs/ conn /edid
the output's EDID, binary.
.PD
.SS Rendering
Present when the device can render (virgl or context init).
.TF caps/id
.TP
.BI caps/ id
the capability set
.I id
(1 virgl, 2 virgl2, 4 venus, ...) as the device describes it,
binary, latest version.
.TP
.B clone
opening it creates a context and returns its
.B ctl
file, as with
.IR ip (3)'s
clone files.
The context lives while any of its files is open.
.PD
.PP
Reading a context's
.B ctl
returns the reply to the last request, first
.IP
.BI ctx " n " seg " name va size"
.PP
the segment that holds the context's command buffers: the client
.IR segattach es
.I name
and writes commands into it.
Requests:
.TF "blob \fImem flags id size\fP"
.TP
.BI "init " "capset \fR[\fPname\fR]"
create the context on the device for capability set
.I capset
(0: the device's default, virgl);
reply
.BI ctx " n " capset " capset" .
.TP
.BI blob " mem flags id size
create a blob resource:
.I mem
is 1 for guest memory, 2 for host memory, 3 for both,
.I flags
1 mappable, 2 shareable, 4 cross device,
.I id
the renderer's object (0: plain shared memory).
Reply
.BI res " r size"
and, for guest memory,
.BI seg " name va" ,
the segment holding it.
.TP
.BI map " r
map host memory into the device's host visible bar; reply
.BI map " r seg barsize offset size info" :
attach
.I seg
.RI ( barsize
bytes) and use
.I size
bytes at
.IR offset .
.I Info
is how the host maps the memory (virtio-gpu's map_info: 1 cached, 2
uncached, 3 write-combining), and
.I seg
the bar's segment of that kind
.RI ( gpu (3)),
so that the processor sees the memory as the host does.
.TP
.BI unmap " r
.TP
.BI unref " r
.TP
.BI attach " r
.TP
.BI detach " r
unmap, release, attach to or detach from the context.
.TP
.BI scanout " r w h stride
show host blob
.I r
on the display instead of the
.IR draw (3)
screen: linear x8r8g8b8 pixels,
.I stride
bytes a row,
.IR w × h
the mode's size; again for every new picture.
.B "scanout 0" ,
a mode change, releasing
.I r
or closing the context shows the screen again.
One context at a time.
.PD
.PP
Writing
.IB "offset length " \fR[\fPring\fR]\fP
to
.B submit
submits that part of the command segment on a timeline
.RI ( ring ,
0 to 63; 0 is the context's CPU timeline); the
.IR k th
submission of a context completes fence
.IR k .
The client must not overwrite it before.
Rings complete in any order; fences of one ring in order.
.PP
Reading
.B fence
returns
.IB "done submitted" :
all fences up to
.I done
are complete.
After writing
.BI "wait " k
(or just
.IR k ),
reads block until fence
.I k
is complete and return
.IB k " done" ;
after
.BI "query " k
they return at once,
.IB k " done"
or
.IB k " busy" .
.B failed
follows if the device rejected a submission.
At most 64 fences of a context can be outstanding.
.SS Intel
.I Intelgpu
drives an Intel GPU of generation 8 (Broadwell) through
.IR gpu (3).
It leaves the display as the firmware or
.IR vga (8)
set it up;
.B ctl
reads the visible plane,
.IP
.BI plane " p gpuaddr stride bpp w h"
.PP
and the context files are those above with other requests.
Each context has an address space of its own, 48 bits (gen 8's
four-level PPGTT; with
.B -3
4 GB in three levels); with
.B -G
all share the global GTT, without isolation.
A buffer object is a segment of pinned pages
.RB ( sticky ,
see
.IR segment (3);
not contiguous) mapped
into it;
.TF "\fLbo\fP \fIsize"
.TP
.BI bo " size \fR[\fPgpuaddr\fR]
creates one, reply
.BI bo " id seg va size gpuaddr" :
the client
.IR segattach es
.I seg
and writes the GPU addresses of its objects into its batches itself.
The driver chooses the address, or the client does
.RI ( gpuaddr ,
page aligned and free;
0 maps the object nowhere), as Mesa's drivers do with softpin.
.TP
.BI bind " id gpuaddr
moves the object to another address (0: nowhere), once the context's
work is done.
.TP
.B map plane
maps the visible plane into the context (uncached, for the display),
reply
.BI plane " id gpuaddr stride bpp w h" ;
.B free
unmaps it.
.TP
.BI free " id
frees it once the context's work is done.
.PD
.PP
Writing
.IB "bo offset " \fR[\fPrcs\fR|\fPbcs\fR]\fP
to
.B submit
starts the batch buffer at
.I offset
in object
.I bo
on the render (default) or blitter engine; it ends with
.BR MI_BATCH_BUFFER_END .
The fences are as above; one that has not completed after 5
seconds is given up and the context marked
.BR failed .
The engines run through execlists, as Linux's i915 has it on gen 8:
each context has a logical ring context (an image of its engine
registers, its page directories among them) and a ring on each
engine; an engine runs one context at a time, the others wait in its
queue, and the context status buffer says when one is done.
Before a context's first batch on the render engine the driver
writes the gen 8 workaround registers into it and runs i915's null
render state, which puts the 3D pipeline into a known state;
.B status
shows how many of those registers hold their value
.RB ( "rcs workarounds" ).
Unused entries of the address spaces point to a scratch page, as in
i915, so that stray accesses of the GPU land there.
.B -R
leaves the workarounds and the render state out,
.B -C
the clock gating workarounds at start (both to find what hangs a
machine).
Fences come from sequence numbers in each context's status page and
the user and context switch interrupts (MSI where the device has it,
.B -p
polls).
An engine that runs one context for 5 seconds is reset, as
.B reset
written to
.B ctl
does it.
Objects are mapped write-back in the shared last level cache, so
that processor and GPU see each other's writes;
.B -U
maps them uncached.
Batches run in their context's address space; still, the driver is
for the host owner.
.IR Intelprobe (8)
and
.B aux/inteltest
check the hardware and the driver.
.SH EXAMPLES
Switch the screen to 1024x768:
.EX
	echo 1024x768 >/dev/gpu/0/outputs/Virtual-1/mode
.EE
.PP
A context, one submission, wait for it, in C (the source tree's
.B tests/gpu/gpuctxtest.c
does more):
.EX
	fd = open("/dev/gpu/0/clone", ORDWR);
	read(fd, buf, sizeof buf);	/* ctx 1 seg gpu0ctx1 0x40000000 0x100000 */
	cmd = segattach(0, "gpu0ctx1", nil, 0);
	fprint(fd, "init 2");
	/* commands at cmd[0..len) */
	fprint(sfd, "0 %d", len);	/* ctx/1/submit */
	fprint(ffd, "1");		/* ctx/1/fence */
	read(ffd, buf, sizeof buf);	/* 1 1 */
.EE
.SS Vulkan
Mesa's venus driver
.RB ( /sys/src/mesa ,
built by the source tree's
.BR scripts/mesa )
uses this interface as its renderer: one context with capability
set 4, its command streams through
.BR submit ,
Vulkan memory as host blobs mapped through the host visible bar,
and its timeline syncs on the fences.
.PP
Vulkan programs present to a window with the instance extension
.BR VK_PLAN9_surface :
.B vkCreatePlan9SurfacePLAN9
takes a
.B VkPlan9SurfaceCreateInfoPLAN9
whose
.B pWindowName
names the window's image; nil means the process's window
.RB ( /dev/winname ),
or the screen without
.IR rio (1).
The swapchain's images are the device's; at every present they are
copied into host visible memory and from there into the window through
a
.IR draw (3)
connection of the driver's own.
On the screen without
.IR rio ,
the host visible copy itself goes to the display
.RB ( scanout ),
no copy by the processor, one image more in the swapchain for the one
shown; the
.I draw
screen is not drawn meanwhile.
.B WSI_PLAN9_COPY=1
in the environment always copies.
The window's size is the surface's; when it changes, presents return
.BR VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR .
.PP
There are no shared libraries: programs link
.BR /$objtype/lib.gcc/ape/libvulkan.a ,
built from
.B /sys/src/mesa
.RB ( build.json ,
the Vulkan headers in
.BR /sys/include/ape/vulkan ),
the driver with a static loader in front of it, and APE's
.IR draw (2)
libraries
.RB ( -lvulkan " " -ldraw " " -l9 " " -lfmt " " -lutf ).
The loader keeps its dispatch tables in the first word of the
dispatchable objects, as Khronos' loader does, and has no layers.
Its functions are
.BR vkl_CreateInstance ,
etc.; the
.B vk
names are weak aliases of them, so a program that keeps its own
function pointers under those names (where it would
.I dlopen
.BR libvulkan.so.1 )
starts from
.B vkl_GetInstanceProcAddr
instead.
Khronos'
.I vkcube
and
.I vulkaninfo
are ported this way.
